\begin{remark}[what is and is not claimed]\label{rem:claims}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \emph{Infimum, not $o(1)$, for one parameter tuple.} At fixed $d$ the value $(1+3/(d+1))/\sqrt2$ is an infimum over fixed
  $(k,\eta_0)$. No single parameter tuple attains it, and Theorem~\ref{thm:main} does not claim $L_N[1/2,(1+3/(d+1))/\sqrt2+o(1)]$ for one
  tuple. That would require letting $k=k(N)\to\infty$. Our constants depend on $k$ through factors
  $(\log N)^{O(k)}$, $\binom{2k}{k}$ and thresholds $N_0(k)$, and we have not carried out such a
  diagonalisation. At $d=9$ the first parameter choice below $1$ ($k=13$) has $c\approx0.9972+\eta_0/\sqrt2$; larger fixed $k$ give smaller constants (Table~\ref{tab:constants}), but none attains $0.9192$.
  One algorithm that schedules many tuples of the same degree does attain it as a genuine $+o(1)$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:P}).
\item \emph{Complete factorisation.} The algorithm outputs the full factorisation, with every prime
  certified (AKS \cite{aks2004}). Prime powers, perfect powers and small prime factors are handled before the
  main splitting routine. The splitting routine is applied at most $\log_2N$ times
  (Section~\ref{sec:uniform}).
\item \emph{No hypotheses, no exceptional set.} Nothing is assumed beyond published theorems. In
  particular no Riemann hypothesis, no hyperelliptic curve method (whose published proof trail is
  incomplete), and no exceptional set of $N$. Every input is a published, numbered statement. One of them,
  Drappeau's Lemme~5, has a published proof that points to computations in Harper's unpublished preprint
  (Appendix~\ref{app:85}). The threshold beyond which the time bound holds involves Stark's, Landau's and
  Lagarias--Odlyzko's effective constants; it is enormous, and effective provided the constants of Drappeau's Lemme~5 are
  (which we believe but no published source states). The algorithm itself uses no analytic constant (Remark~\ref{rem:noN0}),
  so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is unaffected either way. The bound holds for every $N$, not on average.
