+REJECT — trusted statement layer, commit 8bf9d980289b55ba6f35309807d35c8f5d249234.
+
+I reviewed the definitions, explicit hypotheses, and all 57 MAIN theorem statements in [leanA/Challenge.lean](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean), against paper2/main.tex and the specified notes. I did not review the proof bodies. The Challenge, both definition files, paper, and specified notes match HEAD; the pre-existing modifications in two proof files were left alone. All audit artifacts are under codex/scratch/leanA_1/.
+
+There is a proved false published-input premise: CEP. Consequently every theorem taking hCEP is vacuous. Independently, the final declarations are bounds on supplied numerical functions, with no formal connection to an algorithm, an output, or actual expected running time. That second issue persists after repairing CEP. The numerical assembly results can be useful with an accurately described scope; they do not establish the paper's algorithmic conclusions.
+
+1. **FATAL — CEP is false, so the principal conditional conclusions have an impossible premise.**
+
+   **Lean:** [FactoringA.CEP](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:859). Affected declarations: relation_supply, descent_supply, theoremA, corollaryA, theoremRA, theoremRA_opt, corollaryP.
+
+   **Paper:** Fact fact:CEP, §sec:notation; the intended smooth-number range used by Lemma lem:local(iv), equation eq:rhosizes, and Theorem thm:Dk. The original is the corollary following Theorem 3.1 in [Canfield–Erdős–Pomerance, pp. 14–15](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper39.pdf).
+
+   The Lean definition quantifies over every real x. There is no lower bound on x. Lean's Real.log satisfies log(-z) = log z, so the displayed u-range admits points outside the mathematical logarithm's intended domain.
+
+   Here is a counterexample for every proposed U₀. Set ε = δ = 1/2, choose u ≥ max(U₀, 1), put t = 1/(4u), and put x = exp(-exp(-t)). Then 0 < x < 1, so Psi x y = 0 for every y. But in Lean,
+   
+       log x = -exp(-t),
+       log(log x) = -t,
+       (1-ε) log x / log(log x) = exp(-t)/(2t)
+                                            = 2u exp(-1/(4u)) ≥ u.
+
+   Thus both premises of the universally quantified inequality hold. Its lower bound x u^(-u(1+δ)) is strictly positive, contradicting Psi = 0. This uses positive x and positive u; it does not depend on negative real powers or on division by zero.
+
+   **Verification:** [RepositoryCounterexample.lean](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/leanA_1/RepositoryCounterexample.lean) proves ¬ FactoringA.CEP against the repository definition, and also proves CEP → Q for an arbitrary proposition Q. It imports the existing module for its definition; no repository proof was inspected. Lean 4.35.0 accepts the file and reports only propext, choice, Quot.sound, without sorryAx. A second standalone file copies just Psi and CEP and proves the same contradiction.
+
+   **Fix:** require a genuinely large-x domain, for example x ≥ exp(exp 1), and require U₀ ≥ 3; alternatively introduce both X₀ and U₀ and quantify only over x ≥ X₀ and u ≥ U₀, with X₀ ≥ exp(exp 1). Keep ε,δ and the threshold quantifiers in the present uniform order. Merely adding x > 0 is insufficient. Even x > e leaves an unintended branch arbitrarily close to e, where log x/log log x is unbounded while x stays bounded. Regenerate Challenge after fixing the source definition and recheck all consequences without the old premise.
+
+2. **FATAL for the paper's algorithmic claims — the main conclusions have no algorithm or runtime semantics, and admit zero-cost models.**
+
+   **Lean:** [CostModelA](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:929), [CostModelRA](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:1010), theoremA, corollaryA, theoremRA, theoremRA_opt, corollaryP. Relevant fields include factorCost, splitCost, Fsup, TLP.
+
+   **Paper:** Theorem thm:main, Corollary cor:main, Theorem thm:RA, Corollary cor:P; Algorithms alg:factor and alg:split; §sec:uniform.
+
+   The records contain real-valued functions. Their docstrings call these expected costs, but their types contain no procedure, random computation, output distribution, cost random variable, or assertion that the output is a prime factorisation. There is no theorem that an appropriate model of the actual algorithm exists. A finite real-valued function also cannot represent infinite expected runtime.
+
+   Concretely, for fixed admissible P and a > 0, set all cost functions in CostModelA to zero, pTrial = 1, rej = ∅, and Mrows = 1. For sufficiently large N, these satisfy CostInterfaceA with C = 1: probabilities and their averages are at most 1, the rejection bound is nonnegative, the row upper bound is at least log N ≥ 1, and every cost inequality is automatic. The final factorCost inequality has zero left side and nonnegative right side. LenstraPomerance (fun _ => 0) also holds.
+
+   Similarly, CostModelRA with Fsup = tau = nonO = Tsq = Tpost = 0, pTrial = 1, rej = ∅, Mrows = 1 satisfies its interface. Setting the portfolio's T = 0 satisfies hport. These are demonstrations that the *cost interfaces* are independently inhabited by trivial data; because finding 1 makes CEP false, I am not claiming that the complete current hypothesis set has an inhabitant.
+
+   These models need not return anything. Consequently a bound on their fields cannot establish even existence of a factoring algorithm, let alone Las Vegas correctness or complete factorisation. Adding nonnegativity alone would leave these models intact.
+
+   **Fix:** either rename and describe the results as numerical cost-assembly lemmas, or introduce an algorithm/computation object and bind every advertised runtime to its actual cost distribution. State prime-factorisation correctness and almost-sure termination, and derive finite expected costs. A conditional development may place some of these facts in explicit semantic hypotheses, but must state them. They cannot be supplied by field comments.
+
+3. **MAJOR — the cost interfaces assume the main algorithmic bridges, without their essential semantic prerequisites.**
+
+   **Lean:** [CostInterfaceA.bounds](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:953), [CostInterfaceRA.bounds](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:1026); harmonic_mean_identity and split_cost_bookkeeping.
+
+   **Paper:** Proposition prop:select, equation eq:Mrows, Proposition prop:BS, Proposition prop:lv6, Proposition prop:cost, Lemma lem:reject, §sec:uniform, and Lemma lem:generala.
+
+   Numerically, the interface inequalities largely have the paper's expected shapes. However:
+
+   - pTrial is constrained only from below by a geometric event probability. It is not the probability of any formal trial, and is not required to be ≤ 1. An arbitrarily large pTrial makes the division-based cost assumptions arbitrarily strong.
+   - rej is an arbitrary small subset of raw fields. Nothing identifies it with the paper's rejection event or says an unrejected field passes the algebraic checks. Taking rej = ∅ is permitted regardless of those checks.
+   - Mrows need only satisfy 1 ≤ Mrows and an upper bound. The paper requires at least C_col + 12 rows for post-processing. There is no C_col, column system, or sufficient-row prerequisite in these statements.
+   - The interface directly assumes splitCost ≤ 3(Mrows Ctrial + Tpost)/pTrial. The paper obtains this from iid selection trials, the retained-field law, conditional cost bounds, a valid nonempty square dependency, transcript invariance, and Dixon descent. The standalone harmonic-mean and bookkeeping lemmas prove numerical identities/inequalities, not those facts about SPLIT.
+   - The final clause of CostInterfaceA.bounds already assumes the complete factor-tree/runtime inequality for factorCost. It is not a consequence of any formal FACTOR procedure here.
+
+   These are substantial additional internal hypotheses beyond FirstMoment and SecondMoment. The interface does not literally assume the final displayed L-bound, but it assumes most of the algorithmic work required to turn geometric probabilities into such a bound.
+
+   **Fix:** connect pTrial to an actual trial law (or certify it as a lower bound on that law), require 0 ≤ pTrial ≤ 1, define rej through the actual checks, and specify a sufficient row budget and the post-processing output contract. Formulate the conditional cost and transcript hypotheses used by selection. Then prove the split/tree inequalities, or list them explicitly as separate algorithmic hypotheses instead of suggesting that the standalone counting/bookkeeping results establish them.
+
+4. **MAJOR — LenstraPomerance is a rate property of arbitrary T, not the cited existence theorem.**
+
+   **Lean:** [LenstraPomerance](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:921), CostModelA.TLP, CostInterfaceA.bounds's Tshape inequality, theoremA and corollaryA.
+
+   **Paper:** Fact fact:LP, Proposition prop:cost. The published [Lenstra–Pomerance theorem, p. 483](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf) asserts existence of a probabilistic complete-factorisation algorithm with the expected-time bound.
+
+   The Lean definition is exactly LBound (1/2) 1 T. It does not provide that algorithm, correctness, termination, a runtime bound for a specified algorithm, or the claimed interpretation of T as the maximum over inputs ≤ n. It holds for T = 0 and for negative functions. This proposition is not intrinsically inconsistent; its name and PUBLISHED INPUT description overstate its content.
+
+   Taking the maximum of the genuine algorithm's costs over inputs ≤ n is a valid asymptotic reformulation once that runtime function is identified and known finite. Neither the maximum nor the identification occurs in this declaration. The Tshape comparison is another independent interface assumption.
+
+   **Fix:** rename this numerical predicate LPCostBound and separately supply a semantic LP witness with certified complete factorisation, almost-sure termination, and the appropriate expected costs. Define its maximum-cost function and derive LPCostBound. If it remains a trusted published input, state the witness and its semantics in that input. Also correct the source pin: the front-page theorem in the accessed original is labelled “Theorem,” not “Theorem 1.1.”
+
+5. **MAJOR — recursive finiteness and the oracle-to-recursion bridge are premises, not conclusions.**
+
+   **Lean:** [CostModelRA.Fsup](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:1012), CostInterfaceRA.bounds (especially its last two clauses), theoremRA and theoremRA_opt.
+
+   **Paper:** Lemmas lem:OS, lem:waldrec, lem:Trec, lem:generala(A1)–(A3), and the “Finiteness,” “Recursion,” and “Correctness” parts of Theorem thm:RA; notes/recurse_results.md §1.
+
+   The real-valued Fsup is assumed finite, nonnegative, and monotone. The full recurrence for this same Fsup is then supplied as a hypothesis. The theorem solves that recurrence, but does not prove that recursive FACTOR terminates, has finite expectation, returns canonical prime factors, or has Fsup as its runtime supremum. In particular, finiteness of actual recursive calls cannot be obtained merely by choosing a finite codomain for an unrelated function.
+
+   The statements contain no admissible-oracle specification, fresh-coin contract, outer-transcript invariance, or adaptive-call expectation lemma applied to a recursive program. Thus the paper's self-substitution argument is outside the formalised connection.
+
+   **Fix:** specify recursive computation, allow infinite expected cost until finiteness is proved, establish finiteness by the strong induction and trial-division race, and derive the recurrence using admissible canonical factorisation oracles and the adaptive-call bound. Otherwise retain the present result under a name such as recursive_cost_recurrence_bound, with the algorithmic application stated separately and conditionally.
+
+6. **MAJOR — Corollary P assumes the scheduler's crucial estimate and has no computable schedule.**
+
+   **Lean:** [portfolio_assembly](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:838), [corollaryP](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:1077), particularly hport; restart_series.
+
+   **Paper:** Corollary cor:P and its “Restart schedule” and “Bound” paragraphs; Remark rem:claims(a).
+
+   hport supplies, for every fixed j, the eventual inequality T(N) ≤ K_j (log N)^K_j (M j).Fsup N. Together with the individual rate bounds and c_j → 0, this is exactly the missing domination input for the final analytic limit. No scheduler, truncation semantics, independent restart coins, certified output selection, or computability of j ↦ A_j is stated. The family of arbitrary models need not encode a computable family of algorithms. restart_series proves the deterministic series estimate only; it does not derive hport using Markov's inequality for the stated scheduler.
+
+   Also absent is the second conclusion of cor:P: a single nonrecursive algorithm at fixed d = 9 attaining (1 + 3/10)/√2 = 13√2/20 as a genuine +o(1). The fixed-degree parameter-existence lemmas alone do not produce that algorithm.
+
+   There is no error in the analytic quantifier order: fixing j before taking N → ∞ is sufficient, and uniformity in j is unnecessary. The omission is the algorithmic scheduler and its domination theorem.
+
+   **Fix:** formulate a restart-schedule theorem for a computable family of correct Las Vegas algorithms with finite expected runtimes, derive hport from it, and instantiate it both for the recursive degree sequence and the fixed-degree nonrecursive sequence. With the current scope, label corollaryP as a conditional portfolio rate-transfer lemma.
+
+7. **MAJOR — the general-a DL assembly hypothesis drops the batch-smoothness B term.**
+
+   **Lean:** [theoremDL_assembly](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:1111), specifically hC.
+
+   **Paper:** Proposition prop:BS, Lemma lem:generala, and the proof of Theorem thm:DL following Proposition prop:XK.
+
+   hC assumes Ctrial ≤ L[1/2, max(1/√2, θ/(2a)) + o(1)]. At a general a > 0, the paper supplies a batch/search cost Õ(B + S²), so the available trial bound has exponent max(a, 1/√2, θ/(2a)). The paper explicitly retains the resulting row-cost term 2a and only then absorbs it into post-processing's 3a.
+
+   For example θ = 1/4 and a = 1 give a paper-supplied trial exponent 1, whereas hC demands 1/√2. The stronger Lean premise does not follow from the cited general-a estimate. This does not falsify the arithmetic implication. It prevents the claimed general-a application from using the actual supplied bound. The optimising a values remain in a range where this omission is harmless.
+
+   **Fix:** replace hC's constant by max a (max (1/√2) (θ/(2a))). The conclusion can remain costExpDL θ a, since the extra numerator exponent 2a is ≤ 3a for a > 0.
+
+   The theorem is correctly labelled an assembly step. It proves no logarithm computation or correctness statement. In addition, the current FirstMoment/SecondMoment inputs cannot themselves supply prime-modulus DL relation estimates: their SplitInput excludes primes via ¬ IsPrimePow. A full DL theorem needs the prime-modulus transfer/supply statements, not an application of those existing restricted hypotheses to N = p.
+
+8. **MINOR — Yj's natural subtraction changes the small-input definition.**
+
+   **Lean:** [Params.jJ and Params.Yj](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/leanA/Challenge.lean:276), Jset.
+
+   **Paper:** §sec:params, Y = 2^(j−1); Algorithm alg:split's structural guards.
+
+   If j = 0, natural subtraction computes j−1 = 0, so Lean gives Y = 1 rather than 1/2. For N = 2 and admissible k, X = 1 and j = 0; Lean's Jset is {2}, while the literal paper window (1/2, 1] contains no primes. This is a totalisation difference, not a flaw in the large-N estimates: eventually j ≥ 1, and the paper guards tiny inputs.
+
+   **Fix:** explicitly specify this as a guarded small-input convention and restrict paper-identification lemmas to j ≥ 1, or use a rational/real power definition and define the prime window accordingly. Do not describe the natural-valued definition as literally identical for all N.
+
+The remaining statement checks are substantive. I found no additional transcription fault in the following groups; this is a statement comparison, not an endorsement or proof of the internal analytic hypotheses.
+
+| Objects checked | Comparison with the cited source |
+| --- | --- |
+| Lam, LN, LBound | The max(1, log log x) convention matches Lemma lem:F and lv5unif §10; it changes only bounded small inputs relative to the introductory L-notation. LBound is a one-sided asymptotic upper bound: ∀ε>0, ∃N₀, ∀N≥N₀, f N ≤ LN α (c+ε) N. It does not assert a matching lower bound, positivity, or runtime semantics. |
+| Params.ν, hStar, h, θ, Admissible, cA, cRAstar | Match eq:theta, §sec:params, thm:main and thm:RA. Degree subtraction in hStar and θ occurs after coercion to ℚ, so there is no natural-subtraction error there. Admissibility gives k > 0, ν > 0 and θ > 0 in the relevant applications. |
+| thetaMin, muOpt and the 16 Exponents MAIN statements | The branch boundary 4k < d²+1 and both formulas match lem:optmu in the paper's parameter range. Rational division, exact decimal constants, and portfolio D/4 are used correctly. The infimum/existence statements keep parameters fixed before N varies. size_model_floor is only the displayed scalar inequality equivalence, not an existential theorem about admissible growing parameter sequences. |
+| d9_k13, d9_k_le_12 | Match tab:constants: μ=17/156, h*=1/26, θ=16/39+η₀, and the exact strict slack threshold √2−1−16/39. The k≤12 theorem is the correct necessary lower threshold for admissible degree-9 tuples. The k=13 theorem does not assert c<1 for every η₀≤1/100; it correctly uses an iff with the smaller threshold. |
+| Bsm, Ssz, Xsp, jJ in its eventual range, Vl, Vmax, Mb, Hb, Wb, XF, Xr, rhoR, rhoF | Match §sec:params and lem:generala, including ceilings for S,W and floors for B,X,V,M,H. XF uses C′_d=2^(2d+5)+2d; Xr is real division by Y, not natural integer division. No erroneous factor of ℓ or Y was found. |
+| SplitInput, gSet, fields | Match the raw-law design in def:field and the rough odd non-prime-power inputs of §sec:K. ((M+1)/2) in ℤ is the correct ceiling of M/2 for nonnegative M. The raw law intentionally includes fields that later exit/reject; filtering those out here would change FM/LD. Uniform law on the finite product gives the stated independence when nonempty. prob/expect of the empty set are zero by convention, not probability laws, but the large-N input range is intended to make the sampling sets nonempty. |
+| cands, countN, gammaD, descPrimes, descentProb | Match §sec:candidates and the geometric event used in thm:K. The prime-b window is strict at V/2 and closed at V; |a|≤V; gcd=1. Int.toNat is only used with b≥1. countN retains ℓ∣G, the MV/32 lower filter, smooth nonzero G/ℓ and F_A, and the ℓ∣N zero override. Division in ℤ is exact on the divisibility filter. The upper |G| filter is automatic on the field/candidate support. descentProb samples the whole [1,N−1], uses absolute values for both halves, and permits at most k distinct primes, which is closed under taking divisors. It is an event probability, not a defined running procedure. |
+| gbDigit, gbNext, gbT, gbCoeff, gbForm, Gform, FA | Match lem:GB and def:field. Mathlib valMinAbs uses (−g₁/2,g₁/2], including the intended positive tie at even modulus. natAbs is safe under g₁≥2. The recursion uses indices d,…,1, a₀=T₀, and exact division under coprimality. The perturbation uses x^(d−1−i)y^i, while the base form uses x^i y^(d−i), as in the paper. There is no reversed-basis bug. |
+| PhiK, vP, cube; kern_kernel, kernU_count, kernU_prob, kern_prob; pairs and pairs_count | Match lem:kern(ii), its final probability bound, lem:pairs, and Kern-U in lv5unif §3. The guarded negative indices of the kernel recurrence are correct. Integer division in Kern-U count has positive denominator bb′. The probability hypothesis is bb′d≤2H+1, and the exponent is d−2. Kern_prob omits the paper's intermediate wedge-norm bound and kern(i)'s image-lattice statement, but states the final bound used in the claimed assembly. |
+| IsSmooth, Psi, rho, primeCountAP, primesIoc | Smoothness excludes zero and allows negative integers via divisibility of signed integers, equivalently of their absolute values. Psi counts positive integers up to x; primeCountAP counts primes ≤x with a congruence, with no prime-counting off-by-one. These correct total definitions help expose CEP's missing domain. |
+| BrunTitchmarsh | Its positive x,y, q,a, gcd(a,q)=1 and y>q hypotheses and strict constant-2 interval bound match fact:BT and [Montgomery–Vaughan, Theorem 2, p. 121](https://personal.science.psu.edu/rcv4/personal/Publications/large_sieve.pdf). There is no q=0 or nonreduced-residue false premise. |
+| RosserSchoenfeld | Both inequalities, the strict signs, and ranges z>1 and z≥20.5 match fact:RS. Nat.primeCounting is the ≤n version, so the floors preserve the real endpoints. The published original was not independently retrieved successfully in this audit; I do not claim a new primary-source verification of its two constants beyond comparison with the supplied fact. No internal contradiction was found. |
+| FirstMoment and SecondMoment | Their objects and joint (A,ℓ) law match thm:FM and thm:LD; they are averaged over ℓ, not per ℓ. The factorial moment subtracts 1 after coercion to ℝ, so no natural subtraction slip occurs. Thresholds and log-power exponents may depend on fixed P,a but not N. Restriction to SplitInput matches the factoring use following §sec:K; it is insufficient for a prime-modulus DL reuse without transfer. ∃C∈ℝ rather than C≥0 is harmless eventually: C can be enlarged after log N≥1. Neither premise is made trivially true by a moving threshold, a moving exponent, or an empty eventual split-input domain. Their mathematical truth remains an internal hypothesis, not an established published input. |
+| The four Fruitfulness MAIN statements, including dixon_descent | dixon_descent is a correct finite unit-fibre counting implication: S and Y depend on the fixed squares and independent coins, not on a root choice; S is nonempty; X²=Y² holds on the fibre. If R has no root, both cardinalities are zero, so it should only be read as a conditional probability after nonemptiness is supplied. It establishes the needed proper-gcd proportion, not the paper's stronger explicit law asserting uniformity of X/Y on μ₂, nor the algorithm's measurable-transcript specialisation in rem:T/lem:Trec. There is no improper unconditional claim that accepted roots are uniform among all units. |
+| The five SplitCounting MAIN statements | Match thm:S and thm:Sprime's constants and the finite geometric core of thm:K. complete_descent_prob deliberately proves a sufficient event for every shortest vector; this is safe with a fixed tie rule. Its lower bound uses N in the denominator, which is weaker than using N−1 for the sampling interval and is valid. It does not prove that an implemented SQ_SEARCH returns a row; that bridge is an interface assumption. |
+| The six Moments MAIN statements | Reverse Markov, PZ, relation_supply_from_moments, harmonic_mean_identity, split_cost_bookkeeping, residue_spreading have the intended numerical forms. PZ uses E[X(X−1)]+EX and requires EX>0, not EX≥1. The relation bound 1/(P³+P/m) works even when m is smaller than any negative log-power. The harmonic identity correctly ignores zero-probability kept fields, where Lean's 1/0=0 would otherwise be misleading. residue_spreading is the explicit numerical part of lem:W: t≥1, t<W, W≥21, log W≤2log N and log(W/t)≥(η₀/3)log N give the stated 24/η₀ constant. The forced-residue expectation consequence is not stated here, but no false unrestricted residue claim is made. |
+| lemmaF, lemmaF_logfactor | Match lem:F and lv5unif §10 at the level of numerical recurrences. They impose 0<α<1, nonnegative p,δ, a nonnegative finite monotone F, and a positive η margin; K may depend on η and the supplied functions. The K factor can be absorbed into an arbitrarily small additional exponent. hD0 in the log-factor form supplies the sign needed for substituting an upper bound for F(√(2x)). No bound is asserted by substituting η=0. |
+| theoremU_assembly, recursion_factor_two_fifths | Match lv5unif §10 equations (10.1)/(10.3), with an inessential factor 2 difference in front of D absorbed by o(1). The denominator is 1−2^(−2/5), and the numerical bracket is appropriate. The statement does not include a growing-degree design, FM-U/LD-U, the values s=2κ(1+Cγ), σ=2θ₀/(5a), or a factoring algorithm. Its docstring properly says assembly only. |
+| relation_supply, descent_supply, omega_le | Their stated bounds agree with thm:Dk, prop:select(i)'s geometric ingredient, and lem:omega. Uniformity is over all sufficiently large SplitInput integers, and descent_supply's threshold is independent of the subsequently quantified rejection set. These statements are currently vacuous through CEP, as recorded in finding 1. |
+| The seven MainTheorems MAIN statements | Their algebraic rates and optimiser constants agree with the displayed formulas in thm:main, thm:RA, cor:P, and thm:DL. Their exact logical content and omitted algorithmic conclusions are described below and in findings 2–7. |
+
+What the final statements actually establish, read literally:
+
+- theoremA: for the supplied P and M, if the listed published-rate, moment, and cost-interface propositions hold, then ∀ε>0, eventually M.factorCost N ≤ LN (1/2) (P.cA+ε) N. It neither constructs FACTOR nor states any output correctness.
+- corollaryA: for a supplied family of models with the uniform-in-P hypotheses, each c>1/√2 admits a fixed admissible P and a finite real K such that (M P).factorCost N ≤ K LN (1/2) c N for every N≥2. P and K precede N, as required. This does not claim a single tuple attains the infimum. The stronger hLP quantification over even inadmissible P is unnecessary but can be removed by adding an admissibility guard.
+- theoremRA: a supplied real-valued Fsup satisfying the interface has the rate costExpRA θ a. theoremRA_opt specialises the algebraic optimiser. Neither proves finiteness or correctness of a recursively defined algorithm.
+- corollaryP: a supplied T dominated as in hport by the supplied family of rate-bounded real functions has LBound (1/2) 0 T. It supplies no computable scheduler or algorithm.
+- theoremU_assembly and theoremDL_assembly: abstract recurrence/product/quotient upper-bound implications. The former does not establish the growing-degree hypothesis chain; the latter does not state the discrete-logarithm input, output, or equation being solved.
+
+All thresholds in LBound, FM, LD, relation_supply and the final rates can depend on fixed parameters and the supplied functions. None of these statements justifies letting a single tuple depend on N, or obtaining a rate of o(1) uniform in degree/k. Conversely, the fixed-j portfolio limit does not need such uniformity. Those quantifier choices are appropriate.
+
+The complete current hypothesis set is inconsistent because CEP alone is refutable. Passing it as a Prop argument instead of declaring an axiom preserves kernel soundness: Lean can prove a false-premise implication. It does not make that implication evidence for factoring. After repairing CEP, the moment formulations show no additional transcription contradiction in this statement audit, but their truth and a faithful semantic instantiation of the cost interfaces still require independent work.
+

